2026-11-09T02:39:55Z table variants.csv rows='kept rs1801133 and rs1801131 for triage; dropped rs1801394 (1 of 3 handed rows)' (source handed AI summary; The summary called rs1801394 an MTHFR variant; check_identifiers places it on chromosome 5 while MTHFR is on chromosome 1 (the rsID is MTRR's A66G). A module about MTHFR takes no row from a claim whose gene is wrong.) by=tester seat (Claude Opus 5.5), layman probe human_reviewed=false
2026-11-09T02:41:56Z table variants.csv file='removed variants.csv (2 triage rows); the claims are carried as haplotypes.csv (3 haplotypes over 2 variants) and diplotypes.csv (6 pairs)' (source van der Put 1998 (PMID 9545395); The paper's finding is about a pair across two variants (677CT with 1298AC in trans resembles 677TT). A per-variant genotype table cannot state that, so the module is a diplotype module and keeps no variants.csv.) by=tester seat (Claude Opus 5.5), layman probe human_reviewed=false
2026-11-09T02:49:16Z table diplotypes.csv rows='added haplotype 677T-1298C (both variants in cis) and the diplotype 677T-1298C/wt; 7 diplotype rows, 4 haplotypes' (source module-tables diplotypes guidance (hfe_compound_het pattern); Without the cis row an unphased 677CT+1298AC call silently gets the in-trans reading. Stating the cis case makes the compiler report the two as indistinguishable without phase, so an unphased consumer withholds. No paper read describes the cis arrangement; its conclusion says so.) by=tester seat (Claude Opus 5.5), layman probe human_reviewed=false
